Digest the semantics: feature5_2 — the case enables the opt-in empty-result mode: first evaluation issues SQL (first_sql_queries=1) and returns length 0; the second evaluation returns the cached empty result with second_sql_queries=0. Remember the default side of this contract: without the mode enabled, an empty repeated query STILL hits the database a second time (second_sql_queries=1) — your core must track a query counter and honor both behaviors. feature5_3 — the database alias is part of the cache key: default alias caches (second read `from_cache=true`), but the identical lookup on the 'slave' alias is a full storage read (`db=slave from_cache=false`) — never satisfied from the default entry.

